219
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
And so now you're a teacher

220
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and that leaves you your summers

221
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
so that you're able to come down and work at camp.

222
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
It does, which is just kind of a happy coincidence.

223
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Yeah, it wasn't like a master plan,

224
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:05,000
No, it was not a master plan,

225
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
It just kind of worked out that way.

226
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
So I'm a scout master

227
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and I come down here with my scouts.

228
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:15,000
I've got a little bit more of an in or a background

229
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
than a lot of people do, because I've been here,

230
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:18,000
I served on staff,

231
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,000
I've been doing it for a number of years

232
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And what I'm curious about-

233
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and I think what everybody else is curious about-

234
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
is the first couple of times that, as an adult,

235
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
we go and participate in a summer program,

236
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
It can be a little stressful,

237
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
and mostly because we just really don't know what to expect

238
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and we don't know what happens.

239
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
How do you handle that as a program director?

240
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:42,000
What are you doing?

241
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
What are you looking for?

242
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
What makes for a really good relationship

243
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
between you and scout leaders

244
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
that enables their scouts to experience camp

245
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:56,000
to get the most they can out of being here for a week?

246
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Well, I think you said the answer in the question

247
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
of reminding or just remembering

248
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
that the week is here for the scouts.

249
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
The adults are here for supervision of the scouts,

250
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:09,000
but they're at the week,

251
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and we set up the program with the scouts in mind,

252
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
not the adult leaders.

253
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
So keeping that, then, in the back of your head

254
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
of make sure that the scouts are organized enough

255
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
to get to the activities we're providing

256
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
is really the key thing.

257
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
And even letting the boy leadership really step up to the plate

258
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and helping them out, instead of you taking control

259
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
and speaking directly to the young kids,

260
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
let the senior patrol leader do that

261
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
and allow them to have the full camp experience

262
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
without being too hands-on with the scouts.

263
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
You know, you basically just told me

264
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:46,000
how to spend my entire week

265
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and that doesn't need a whole lot of elaboration, really.

266
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
So what don't I do?

267
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
What is like a common mistake?

268
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,000
that just makes things more difficult.

269
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Trying to schedule everything out

270
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
hour by hour before you even come to camp

271
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
makes things very, very difficult.

272
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
We are very open.

273
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
If a staff member of mine comes.

274
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:10,000
Craig, I have a great idea.

275
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
I want to run it this Tuesday.

276
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
I'm going to let them do that

277
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
and having troops already set in such a schedule

278
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:18,000
that then they can't experience

279
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
this really, really great idea

280
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
is just going to limit the amount of fun

281
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
that they can have down a camp

282
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and limit the things that they can experience

283
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
while they're down a camp.

284
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
Most of the program is outside.

285
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Oh yeah, it's absolutely outside.

286
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:35,000
So whatever happens with weather

287
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:38,000
or anything, you've got to adjust to that too.

288
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
Like I said just this week,

289
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
we had Rain on Monday Night,

290
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
which is a big camp-wide activity game for us,

291
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
and we had to move that inside

292
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
the last 10 minutes of starting the game

293
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
and trying to organize all that,

294
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and the Scout seemed to have a really good time,

295
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
but we had a couple troops that

296
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
didn't show up because it was raining

297
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and didn't want to experience that,

298
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and those troops missed out

299
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
on this activity.

300
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
The answer is:

301
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
don't plan

302
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
so much that you're inflexible

303
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
or you're going to be disappointed

304
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
if things change.

305
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
There are things that we have scheduled way in advance

306
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
that don't change.

307
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
So if our enter troop competitions

308
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
like the Rifle Shoot or Archery Shoot

309
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
or Track Meet, by all means

310
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
figure out when those are to make sure

311
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
your guys can participate,

312
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
but don't schedule things so

313
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
strictly that you limit yourself

314
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
to what you can participate in.

315
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
What other things

316
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
would you say? don't do that

317
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
or don't step that way

318
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
or make?

319
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
I think one of the things

320
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
that I see the most is

321
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
a lot of Scout Masters

322
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and adult leaders come down to camp,

323
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
are so used to dealing with

324
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
other adults that they forget

325
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
that some of the people teaching merit badges

326
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
are 15, 16 years old,

327
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
and while I

328
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
can tell you that those counselors aren't going to be perfect

329
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
because they're 15 and 16 years old,

330
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
they are in that position for a reason:

331
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
they're working in the nature lodge,

332
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
they're teaching oceanography

333
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
because they like the badge

334
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
that allows them to teach it.

335
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
so while they might not have a PhD in the subject,

336
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
they are

337
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
knowledgeable in that area.

338
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
so giving them the benefit of the doubt

339
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
instead of kind of jumping in and trying to take

340
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
charge of something

341
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
just because it's not an adult doing it

342
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
is something else that I think

343
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
Scout Masters should try to stay away from a little bit.

344
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
Is it a good idea for me to go around

345
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
and sit in on the merit badge sessions

346
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and stuff like that?

347
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
Sitting in for a whole section,

348
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
you know,

349
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
drive some counselors

350
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
makes them a little bit nervous

351
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
and they're not able to engage as much

352
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
with the Scouts.

353
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
but just a quick pop in,

354
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
listen for five minutes, see what your Scouts are learning

355
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
and move on to the next merit badge

356
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
so you have a better idea of what's going on everywhere

357
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
in camp,

358
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
something that I would probably recommend a little bit more

359
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
than sitting in for a whole section

360
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
in one merit badge.

361
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
So let's say-

362
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and I'm doing what you suggest-

363
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
I'm looking in for a couple minutes here

364
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
just out of curiosity

365
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and things like that,

366
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
or just in my general

367
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
day to day at camp,

368
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
and I

369
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
sense that there's a problem

370
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
that you know a staff member

371
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
maybe isn't living up to what you're expecting

372
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
of them, or I hear

373
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
a little piece of instruction and I'm kind of questioning it

374
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
or something like that.

375
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
What do I do there?

376
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
If it's something that you're not quite sure

377
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
whether or not the staff member

378
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
was saying was accurate,

379
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
I would just go up to them after the merit badge

380
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
and try and find them, just real briefly,

381
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and say: are you sure that's right,

382
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
and I've been doing it this way

383
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
for a while,

384
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
or I understood it is that,

385
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
and just have a conversation with them.

386
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
the more abrupt you are,

387
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
the worse. it goes

388
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
more often.

389
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
but if it really is a concern, you can go to the department head

390
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
or come up to me

391
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
or to the program director in your camp.

392
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
I know we had a concern

393
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
about how a requirement was being

394
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
fulfilled for a camping merit badge

395
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
and it was as simple as

396
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
somebody coming up to me and us

397
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
looking through the requirement booked together

398
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
and figuring it out.

399
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
so a lot of times it's just a miscommunication

400
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
or

401
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
because you're only there for one day

402
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and going back to the complete opposite of what I just said a second ago:

403
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
being there for just one day,

404
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
you do miss some of those other things that get done.

405
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
so understanding that the merit badge

406
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
is a week long process

407
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
and not just that short time

408
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
that you might have been in the merit badge.

409
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
so here I am. when I come down to camp

410
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
I'm working with my scouts

411
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
and everything, but at the same time

412
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
I kind of want to contribute to what's going on.

413
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
I maybe want to contribute to camp,

414
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
you know, help out with things

415
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
or something like that.

416
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
what are my options there? what can I do?

417
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
a great option is the first year camper program.

418
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
getting involved

419
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
and letting your young scouts know

420
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
that your skills are also there.

421
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and just seeing what they're doing

422
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
in camp, seeing what they're learning

423
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
at the first age, can help you better understand

424
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
what might be going on in their heads

425
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
through the next seven, eight years

426
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
that they're in scouting.

427
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
so doing that is a great idea.

428
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
working through that program,

429
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
because they also get around to doing

430
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
pretty much everywhere in camp.

431
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
they'll go up to the cope course, the rifle range,

432
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
visit, every part in camp.

433
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
so working with that program might be a good idea.

434
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
also,

435
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
I know we have a list of service projects

436
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
that adult leaders say,

437
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
hey, we're looking to do something.

438
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
is there anything we can do?

439
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
and if it happens that one of them is a big woodworker,

440
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
we might ask them to make a new sign for somewhere

441
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
and a lot of times just by walking

442
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
around camp scoutmasters get an idea

443
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
of. do you know what

444
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
that looks like- something I can tackle all by myself,

445
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
and let's see what I can do.

446
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
let's talk a little bit specifically

447
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
about the staff experience.

448
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
I think we got a pretty good idea

449
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
of what that was like when you were younger.

450
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
what are your direct responsibilities right now?

451
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
I mean, we know,

452
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
you know basically somebody

453
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
comes to camp and

454
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
is introduced to the program director.

455
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
what does that guy do?

456
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
most of the stuff that I do happen during staff week,

457
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
before any of the

458
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
scoutmasters actually

459
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
or any of the scouts got into camp at all.

460
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
so during any given week

461
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
the program director is there to,

462
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
in a way, just put out fires,

463
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
like if there are questions about something.

464
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
to make sure that you're on top of it,

465
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
because I think

466
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
I have and I think most program directors

467
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
have a very good understanding of what's going on

468
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
inside the camp at any given time

469
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
in any program area.

470
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
but the thing that I do probably most directly

471
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
is organize the dining hall program,

472
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
making sure messages get out,

473
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
making sure that the songs get sung

474
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
and the camp-wide activities.

475
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
we have three camp-wide games each week

476
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
and I'm the one that puts together those sheets

477
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
to make sure the staff knows what's going on for each of those.

478
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
and I also teach a merit badge

479
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
each morning. I teach a communications merit badge,

480
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
yeah. so

481
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
in addition to that,

482
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
you kind of like the chief morale officer-

483
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
yeah, absolutely,

484
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
we just had a staff movie night

485
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
that we'll go down and we'll watch a movie down there.

486
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
we did a wing bowl

487
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
one night. we do little

488
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
games that- a wing bowl,

489
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
yeah, like who can eat the most chicken wings,

490
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
like that sort of thing.

491
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
so just kind of fun activities that

492
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
I mean. yeah, we all work together,

493
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
but it's work,

494
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
and when we're not working in the evening

